Well, I'm not German (and neither was any of my ancestors during
recorded history), but I still notice that you approach has the severe
limitation that a person who is really good most probably won't be
able to post his or her ascension list.

I'm far from the best nethacker, even if we restrict the scope and
consider only Finnish nethackers, but I still can't say my number of
ascension to the nearest dozen.

I have mistakenly erased the logfile of my main workstation at least
four times (perhaps more, I can't remember) and have ascensions on my
father's three different computers, on three different university
servers, and on two public internet servers (though I can't remember
anymore which ones they were). That's 12 different high-score lists,
seven of whom have already been destroyed. I'm pretty sure that I have
more than 40 ascensions, but beyond that I can't say anything certain.

Also, in my opinion the ascension rate is a far better indicator of
playing skill than the pure number of ascensions. That too is not
perfect since a player who only plays non-conduct valkyries could
conceivably get a rate somewhere close 2/3 without learning how to
play other roles effectively, and there is also a question of how many
games should be taken into that ratio so that it will correspond to
the current skill of the player.

I think that it might be an interesting test if someone organized a
nethack tournament where each entry consists of playing one game in
each role. The score would then be the number of ascensions among the
13 tries (in case of ties the one with the lowest sum of points being
the winner, supposing that the winner has at least one ascension). I
tested this concept by playing two full sets. In the first one I got
three ascensions (barbarian, knight, tourist) and in the second four
(archeologist, knight, samurai, valkyrie). I would guess that the best
players could get 6-7 ascensions, and even more if they get a few
lucky starts.

